Date: Wed, 22 Dec 1999 21:09:58 -0500
From: "Daniel L. Schwartz" <expresso@snip.net>
Subject: RE: NT registry hacks to improve performance
To: AlphaNT Mail List <AlphaNT@AlphaNT.com>
In-Reply-To: <85E591C1EE0BD3119ADD00062905ED2649BFC5@INQUISITOR>
Reply-To: AlphaNT Mail List <AlphaNT@AlphaNT.com>

HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\ControlSet001\Control\Session Manager\Memory
Management
DisablePagingExecutive=1

At 05:06 PM 12/22/99 -0800, you wrote:
>This disallows the kernel from being paged out, reducing the amount of
>memory that the paging code is allowed to use.  With today's modern systems
>this makes very little difference to anything.  This made a big difference
>back in the day when NT defined internally a "LARGE" system to be anything
>with 64MB+ of RAM.
>
>Regards,
>
>Paul Bunn, UltraBac.Com, 425-644-6000
